Sure, for example, loops are used when you want to make more than one of something.  Let’s say we want to make twenty of the same thing.  One way to do it is:

for (i=0; i<20; i++) {
    // code to make something
}

The "for" in "for" stands for "for", "i=", the condition to test for the loop, the integer "i", and the action that will be performed each time the loop runs.  This causes the loop to run 20 times, with "i" starting at zero on each loop.  

Also, loops can be used in with "if/then" statements, or to iterate though a set of objects, like this:

for (object : objects) {
    // doSomething()
}

